---
description: Destructure object props, parameters, and variables in-body when 3+ properties are accessed, to shorten lines and improve readability.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Destructure Single-Object Props

**When to apply**: A function or component accesses 3+ properties of the same object (prop, parameter, or local variable) in its body, with no naming collisions in scope.

## Forbidden Pattern

```tsx
// ❌ Bad — repeated `maintenanceLog.` on every line; hard to scan
const DetailsContent = ({ maintenanceLog }: Props) => {
  return (
    <Grid>
      <Value value={maintenanceLog.fleet ? maintenanceLog.fleet.name : 'Unknown'} />
      <Value value={maintenanceLog.service_date ? formatDate(maintenanceLog.service_date) : 'N/A'} />
      <Value value={maintenanceLog.cost ? maintenanceLog.cost.toLocaleString() : 'N/A'} />
      <Value value={maintenanceLog.service_provider ?? 'N/A'} />
    </Grid>
  )
}
```

## Required Pattern

Destructure at the top of the function body, then use the short names throughout:

```tsx
// ✅ Good — destructure once, use short names everywhere
const DetailsContent = ({ maintenanceLog }: Props) => {
  const { fleet, service_date, cost, service_provider } = maintenanceLog

  return (
    <Grid>
      <Value value={fleet ? fleet.name : 'Unknown'} />
      <Value value={service_date ? formatDate(service_date) : 'N/A'} />
      <Value value={cost ? cost.toLocaleString() : 'N/A'} />
      <Value value={service_provider ?? 'N/A'} />
    </Grid>
  )
}
```

## Key Rules

- Destructure when **3+ properties** of the same object are accessed in the body
- Place the destructuring **at the top** of the function body, before any logic or JSX
- Only destructure fields that are **actually used** — no speculative destructuring
- Keep the original name in the **function signature** for type clarity; destructure in the body, not the parameter list
- **Skip** destructuring when there is a naming collision with other variables already in scope

## Exceptions

- `.map()` / `.filter()` callbacks with **1–2 property accesses** — inline dot access is fine
- When the object name provides **important disambiguation** (e.g., `source.name` vs `target.name` where both are in scope)
- Spread patterns where the rest object is also needed: `const { id, ...rest } = item` is already destructuring — continue the same block
